---
import Learn from "../layouts/Learn.astro";
import * as CH from "./seasonality.charts.js";
const NUM = CH.NUM;
---
<Learn
  title="Seasons in the data · Measure of India"
  description="Seasonality: prices keep a calendar. Vegetables glut every winter and spike some monsoons; the food level dips almost every November to February. A seasonal wobble is not a trend."
  slug="seasonality"
  kicker="Prices: Seasonality"
  h1="A dip on schedule is <i>not a trend.</i>"
  lede="Vegetable prices keep a calendar: the winter glut, the summer climb, the occasional monsoon spike. How to tell the season from the trend, and why winter always looks like relief."
  meta={[
    ["Concept", "Seasonality"],
    ["Illustrated with", "Food CPI level, 2016–2025"],
    ["Source", "MoSPI, NSO"],
    ["Level", "First principles"],
    ["Reading time", "6 min"],
  ]}
  dateStamp="Evergreen"
  crumbTitle="Seasonality"
>

  <!-- § 01 the definition -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The word, defined</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">First principles</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>Vegetable prices keep a calendar. Every winter the new harvest reaches the mandis, supply gluts, and the food price level dips; every summer it climbs back. The rhythm repeats with such regularity that you can nearly read the month off the shape of the chart.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Seasonality <span class="sub">a calendar inside the data, repeats every year</span></div>
          <p>A pattern in a series that repeats at the same time every year: prices, output or employment rising and falling with the seasons rather than with any real change in the economy. Winter's cheap vegetables are seasonality. So is the festival-month spike in gold.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the data has a heartbeat, and the calendar sets its pace.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">None of this is a trend. A winter dip means winter arrived, not that inflation is beaten, and a monsoon spike means the rains failed somewhere, not that prices are out of control. The trap is that both feel like news. Every winter the level falls, and every winter someone calls it a turning point.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>The dips are the calendar, not the trend.</b> The level sags each winter and climbs each summer. Only one of those is the story.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Spikes have dates too.</b> The onion shock of December 2019 sat right inside the seasonal window. A season can host a crisis; it is still a season.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>Festivals are seasons.</b> Demand rises on schedule as surely as supply does, and the data keeps both appointments.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 plate : the calendar drawn ten times -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The calendar, drawn ten times</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Food index level, monthly</span></div>
    <p>Below is the food price level for a full decade, with the four months from November to February shaded every year. The pattern needs no model and no statistics: the line sags inside the grey bands and climbs outside them.</p>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-seas" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The same dip, every winter: the calendar is the pattern</h3>
            <div class="sub">CPI food &amp; beverages, index level, 2012 = 100, shaded: November to February, Jan 2016 to Dec 2025</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-seas" set:html={NUM["pv-seas"]}></div><div class="c">Dec 2025, index level</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-seas" set:html={CH.seas()} />
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: MoSPI, CPI food &amp; beverages group index, base 2012=100, vintage <span id="pf-seas" set:html={NUM["pf-seas"]}></span>, shaded bands: November to February</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://www.mospi.gov.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The grey bands are November to February, every year for a decade. Watch the line inside them: it ends <b id="se-fell" set:html={NUM["se-fell"]}></b> of the shaded windows lower than it began, as the winter harvest comes in. Then watch what it does each summer: it climbs back, and further. The wobble is seasonal; the climb is the trend.</p>
            <p>Across the whole decade the level rose <b id="se-rise" set:html={NUM["se-rise"]}></b>. The winter dips subtracted nothing from that total; they only delayed it. A seasonal fall is a pause with a date on it, and the date is the tell.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">The winter of 2024–25</span>
            <p>The deepest dip of the decade followed the sharpest spike. The food level entered November 2024 at <b id="se-c-nov" set:html={NUM["se-c-nov"]}></b> and left February 2025 at <b id="se-c-feb" set:html={NUM["se-c-feb"]}></b>, a fall of <b id="se-c-drop" set:html={NUM["se-c-drop"]}></b>. Commentators called it victory over inflation. It was winter.</p>
            <p>The line had done the same thing, on the same schedule, eight times before. Relief that arrives with the harvest leaves with the summer, and it did.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 why year-on-year exists -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>Why year-on-year exists</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Cancelling the calendar</span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>There is a way to cancel the calendar: never compare a month with its neighbour. Compare it with itself, one year back. December against December carries the same weather, the same festivals and the same harvest on both sides of the ratio, so the seasonal wobble divides out. What remains is closer to real change.</p>
        <aside class="defbox">
          <div class="term">Year-on-year <span class="sub">y/y, compares like with like</span></div>
          <p>A comparison of one month with the same month a year earlier. Because both sides sit in the same season, the calendar cancels, and the remaining change is the closest thing to a clean read on inflation.</p>
          <div class="eg"><b>In plain words:</b> the only fair fight between two months is the same month, twice.</div>
        </aside>
        <p style="margin-top:1.4em">This is why the headline inflation rate is always a year-on-year figure, and why the month-on-month change, for all its speed, is mostly weather. The companion entry, <a href="/learn/yoy-and-mom/">Year-on-year, month-on-month</a>, takes the two measures apart. The short version: one is slow and honest; the other is fast and mostly noise.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iv</span>
          <b>December versus December.</b> Same season on both sides, so the season cancels. That is the entire trick.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 misreads -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>How to misread this concept</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Prices fell this winter, so inflation is beaten.”</h3>
          <p>Prices fall most winters; that is what winter does to food. Beaten inflation would show up in the year-on-year rate, which compares this winter with the last one. A seasonal dip and a falling rate are different animals, and only one of them is news.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“A festival-month spike is a crisis.”</h3>
          <p>No. Demand keeps a calendar too, and if the same spike arrives every festival season and unwinds right after, it is a season, not a crisis. Check the same month a year back before reaching for the word.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>“Seasonality means the data is wrong.”</h3>
          <p>The opposite. Seasonality means the data is honest enough to show the weather. The error is in the reading, not the recording: a pattern you can predict is not noise, it is information you have not used yet.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div slot="prov-source">
    <h6>Source of record</h6>
    <p>Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation, National Statistical Office. Consumer Price Index group indices on base 2012=100, rural, urban and combined; retrieved via the eSankhyiki API, vintage 2026-08-04. The level shown is the published food &amp; beverages group index.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-revision">
    <h6>Definitions &amp; honesty</h6>
    <p>Seasonality here is shown, not modelled: the shaded bands mark November to February, and the level is the published index with no adjustment applied. Formal seasonal adjustment exists for some series; this page names the pattern and leaves the data raw.</p>
    <p>Where the data cannot answer a question, this page says so rather than inventing precision.</p>
  </div>
  <div slot="prov-note">
    <h6>This note</h6>
    <p>Prices, version 1.0. Evergreen, written to survive the next print. The winter of 2024–25 is used as history, not news; the chart redraws from the baked data on every build.</p>
    <p>Companion guide: <a href="/guides/cpi/">Consumer Price Index</a>.</p>
  </div>

</Learn>
